        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Arab Spring</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> above all; sustained demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, Jordan, Kuwait, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>; minor protests in Djibouti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Tunisian Revolution after Mohamed Bouazizi’s self-immolation (December 2010 – January 2011); the spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> (2011); the first wave faded by mid- to late 2012; then civil wars and the “Arab Winter”; a new wave from 2018, sometimes called the “Second Arab Spring”.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mohamed Bouazizi, the Tunisian street vendor whose self-immolation set off the protests; Tawakkol Karman, Yemeni organiser of peaceful protests and co-recipient of the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a>; Wael Ghonim, Egyptian Internet activist; the April 6 Youth Movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; Suhair Atassi, unofficial spokesperson of the “Syrian revolution”.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ben Ali, Mubarak and Gaddafi fell in 2011 and Saleh in 2012; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> adopted a new constitution in 2014; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>’s elected president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> was overthrown by the military in 2013; the Gulf monarchies held on; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>’s uprising was crushed with Gulf troops; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> descended into civil war.</span></div>
