    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Set off by the self-immolation of the Tunisian vendor Mohamed Bouazizi in December 2010, protests spread across the Arab world under the slogan “the people want to bring down the regime”.</li><li>Within little more than a year the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> had been forced from power, while uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> were met with violent repression.</li><li>Early hopes gave way to counter-revolution and the civil wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, which some call the Arab Winter; analysts tie the different outcomes to the strength of institutions, civil society and the middle class, and to the army’s stance.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name “Arab Spring” was coined by Western commentators: the political scientist Marc Lynch thought he may have coined it unintentionally in a Foreign Policy article of 6 January 2011. Protesters spoke instead of an uprising (intifada), an awakening (sahwa) and revolution (thawra).</p></aside>
    </section>

