    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">2010</span><span class="rf-text">Tunisian street vendor Mohamed Bouazizi sets himself on fire after his wares are confiscated, and protests begin in Sidi Bouzid</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2011</span><span class="rf-text">Zine El Abidine Ben Ali flees <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in January, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak">Hosni Mubarak</a> resigns in February after 18 days of protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2011</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-cooperation-council">Gulf Cooperation Council</a> forces enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> at the government’s request, and the protest camp at Pearl Roundabout is cleared</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2011</span><span class="rf-text">The arrest and abuse of children in Daraa sets off protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> that grow into the Syrian civil war</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2011</span><span class="rf-text">After a UN-authorised no-fly zone and a bombing campaign led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom, rebels take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a> is killed at Sirte</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2012</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a> hands the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> to Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi in exchange for immunity from prosecution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2013</span><span class="rf-text">The Egyptian military overthrows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a>, the president elected in 2012</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2014</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> adopts a new constitution and holds parliamentary and presidential elections, completing its transition to democracy</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The protests grew out of widespread dissatisfaction, above all among the young and the trade unions, with the rule of their governments. The causes cited include human rights violations, political corruption, economic decline, unemployment, extreme poverty and a large share of educated but dissatisfied young people; across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the Gulf, wealth lay in the hands of rulers in power for decades, with little transparency over its redistribution. Some point to wide gaps in income and the Great Recession; others blamed rising food prices on commodity traders and the conversion of crops to ethanol.</p><p>Discontent had been building. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, protests in the mining area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gafsa">Gafsa</a> in 2008 lasted for months. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which had seen more than 3,000 labour actions since 2004, an attempted strike at the state textile factories of al-Mahalla al-Kubra on 6 April 2008 gave rise to the 6 April Committee of youths and labour activists. A leaked American cable of 2008 described daily demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and in November 2010 Moroccan security forces destroyed the Gdeim Izik protest camp of young Sahrawis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>.</p><p>The spark came in Tunisia. On 17 December 2010 a municipal inspector confiscated the wares of Mohamed Bouazizi, who could not find work and sold fruit at a roadside stand; an hour later he set himself on fire. His death on 4 January 2011 brought together the unemployed, activists, unionists, students and lawyers in the Tunisian Revolution.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The demonstrators’ great slogan was ash-shaʻb yurīd isqāṭ an-niẓām, “the people want to bring down the regime”, and on the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the cry was “bread, freedom and social justice”. Their hopes were to end corruption, widen political participation and bring greater economic equity. Protesters spoke of their action not as a “spring” but as an uprising (intifada), an Arab awakening (sahwa) or renaissance (nahda), and of karama, dignity, and thawra, revolution. Some looked to the Turkish model of contested but peaceful elections, a fast-growing economy and a secular constitution under an Islamist government.</p><p>Many began with reform rather than revolution in mind. The April 6 Movement called for “coalition and co-operation between all factions and national forces to reach the reform and the peaceful change of the conditions of Egypt”, and one of its activists recalled that “not a single one of us had expected” the crowds in Tahrir Square to demand the regime’s downfall. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> the protests at first sought political freedom and respect for human rights, not the end of the monarchy.</p><p>Contrary to expectations, the uprisings were not led by Islamists, and there was hardly any central leadership. The sociologist Asef Bayat argues that, unlike the revolutions of the 1960s and 1970s, they “lacked any associated intellectual anchor” and did not aim to capture the state; instead of revolution or reform, he speaks of “refolution”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak.jpg" alt="Hosni Mubarak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak">Hosni Mubarak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hosni_Mubarak_-_Official_Photo_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Protests began in Sidi Bouzid on 18 December 2010, and on 14 January 2011 Zine El Abidine Ben Ali fled to Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, ending 23 years in power. Unrest spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and beyond, its largest demonstrations often held on a Friday “day of rage”. In Egypt protests began on 25 January; the government tried to cut off the Internet, and on 11 February <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak">Hosni Mubarak</a> resigned after 18 days, handing power to the armed forces.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> protesters occupied Pearl Roundabout in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manama">Manama</a> from February; on 14 March <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-cooperation-council">Gulf Cooperation Council</a> forces, mainly Saudi and Emirati troops, arrived at the government’s request, and the camp was cleared. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> protests from 15 February turned to war; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> Resolution 1973 authorised a no-fly zone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom bombed pro-Gaddafi forces, rebels took Tripoli in August, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a> was killed at Sirte on 20 October 2011. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> the arrest and abuse of children in Daraa for writing anti-government slogans set off protests that met harsh clampdowns and grew into the Syrian civil war.</p><p>In Yemen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a> was injured in an assassination attempt in June 2011, signed a Gulf Cooperation Council plan in November and handed power to Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi in February 2012. By mid- to late 2012 the first wave had faded.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi.jpg" alt="Muammar Gaddafi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moamer_el_Gadafi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevan Kragujević, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The uprisings drew in many groups: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> the unemployed, political and human rights activists, trade unionists, students, professors and lawyers; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> computer-literate working-class youths and middle-class students, some calling themselves “the Facebook generation”. Nine in ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and Tunisians in one poll said they used Facebook to organise protests. The Internet activist Wael Ghonim said his Facebook page was a “simple reaction to the events in Tunisia”, with no master plan, and the April 6 Movement was one of the leading forces of the Egyptian uprising. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, mosques were a main platform for coordinating protests.</p><p>Tawakkol Karman, a Yemeni human rights activist and politician who called a “Day of Rage” for 3 February 2011, shared that year’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> for organising peaceful protests, and Time magazine named “The Protester” its Person of the Year. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> the politician Suhair Atassi became an unofficial spokesperson of the “Syrian revolution”. Islamists were present but did not lead: the Muslim Brotherhood’s old guard joined only when pushed by its young members, and Egypt’s mufti, Ali Gomaa, declared rising against Mubarak haram.</p><p>Against them stood rulers long in power: Ben Ali, Mubarak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamad-bin-isa-al-khalifa">Hamad bin Isa Al Khalifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a> of Syria, whose forces met protest with mass arrests, gunfire and tanks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi.jpg" alt="Mohamed Morsi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohamed_Morsi-05-2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wilson Dias/ABr, CC BY 3.0 br</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Outcomes varied greatly. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> a constituent assembly, elected in 2011 with 37% of the vote for the Islamist party Ennahda, prepared a constitution adopted on 26 January 2014 and seen as progressive, and elections that year completed the transition to democracy, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a> since formed there questions that success. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> of the Muslim Brotherhood won the 2012 presidential election, regarded as free and fair, but on 3 July 2013 the military removed him; reports since describe more executions and pro-democracy activists in exile or hiding.</p><p>The monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> co-opted the movement and kept order without significant social change. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> an independent commission found in 2011 that the government had systematically tortured prisoners, and repression went on a decade later; in the United Arab Emirates 69 men were convicted in the “UAE-94” trial after a petition for reform. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> the result was a complete collapse of society: the Syrian civil war killed between about 580,000 and 656,000 people, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> Houthi rebels overthrew the new government in 2015.</p><p>Social scientists link success to strong civil society and institutions, an educated middle class, open media and an army that sided with the protesters, as in Tunisia and Egypt; regimes without major oil wealth or hereditary succession were more likely to fall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The violence and instability that followed is commonly called the Arab Winter: civil wars, regional instability and religious wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia Muslims. Observers such as Quinn Mecham and Tarek Osman note the repression of the Muslim Brotherhood, the rise of Islamist state-building where states failed, most expansively the Islamic State, and sectarianism fed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> proxy conflict; Hussein Agha and Robert Malley call sectarianism “a politically expedient fable”. George Lawson sees a “largely unsuccessful revolution”, and Marwan Kabalan argued in 2019 that counter-revolutionary forces were seeking to resurrect military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p><p>A new wave of protests from 2018, sometimes called the Second Arab Spring, brought the resignation of the prime ministers Haider al-Abadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 2018 and Saad Hariri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in 2020, and the overthrow of Omar al-Bashir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and Abdelaziz Bouteflika of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 2019. In December 2024 a rebel offensive led by Hay’at Tahrir al-Sham brought down the Assad regime after 13 years of civil war, and as of 2025 the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> went on.</p><p>The Lebanese novelist Elias Khoury wrote that “perhaps the secret of the Arab Spring lies not in its victories or defeats, but in its ability to liberate people from fear.”</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Arab Spring was a wave of protests and uprisings that began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in December 2010 after the self-immolation of Mohamed Bouazizi and spread across the Arab world, forcing out the rulers of Tunisia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Its hopes of democracy and social justice were largely overtaken by repression, counter-revolution and civil wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Libya and Yemen, though protests returned from 2018.</em></p>

